WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.250
- A great operator, a great entrepreneur,

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.071
a great leader, a great CEO, like,

00:00:04.071 --> 00:00:05.070
that's what you do.

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:06.310
You work for everybody else.

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:08.340
And when you're a great entrepreneur...

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.630
A CEO is a great leader in that time,

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:13.730
but she can leave in three
years and be a different CEO.

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:16.100
This is my baby for life.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.520
This is why people struggle
with work-life balance

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:19.400
when they're entrepreneurs.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.368
Your business is the next child.

00:00:21.368 --> 00:00:22.361
(upbeat music)

00:00:22.361 --> 00:00:23.460
(cheering)

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.757
You've got your perspective.

00:00:25.757 --> 00:00:28.766
(crowd cheering)

00:00:28.766 --> 00:00:29.599
I just want to be happy.

00:00:29.599 --> 00:00:30.480
Don't you want to be happy?

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.330
- Well, GaryVee, welcome to the A-List.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:37.500
- Thank you so much.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.650
- Now, you have a great
backstory and I feel like

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:41.110
the people who are watching this are going

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:42.040
to be in two camps;

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.900
either, they are just
stark raving mad lunatics

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.278
and followers of you on
every device they own,

00:00:47.278 --> 00:00:50.230
or they're saying, hmm,
who is this young man?

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:53.320
And I don't mean to say that
from an older perspective.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:54.410
- You're absolutely right.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:56.180
- Yeah, so give us some background.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:58.460
And I will say, I now
know, we were talking.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:00.920
I've dpne so much research, that diet.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:01.930
I don't know if you've heard of that diet.

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:03.340
The Whole30 diet.

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:04.173
- Yes.

00:01:04.173 --> 00:01:05.256
- So I've been telling my
family I'm on a Whole Gary diet.

00:01:05.256 --> 00:01:06.089
(laughing)

00:01:06.089 --> 00:01:07.580
And I've been listening
to you so much that

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.830
our 14 year old got in the car yesterday

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:12.080
and there was only like
four seconds of your voice

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:13.728
and he goes, is that GaryVee?

00:01:13.728 --> 00:01:15.025
I've got GaryVee.

00:01:15.025 --> 00:01:15.858
So let's do it.

00:01:15.858 --> 00:01:17.030
So now, he's on the GaryVee Diet.

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:18.667
- I love it.

00:01:18.667 --> 00:01:20.013
Is the cursing okay?

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:22.480
Because I'm on not-televison,

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:24.093
there is some Jersey words in there.

00:01:24.093 --> 00:01:25.060
- Yes, some.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:27.440
Yes, I will just say
that I'm glad you brought

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.680
that up because the only F-word
we use in PBS in funding.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:31.513
- Understood.

00:01:31.513 --> 00:01:32.355
- Yeah.

00:01:32.355 --> 00:01:33.188
(speaking faintly)

00:01:33.188 --> 00:01:35.320
- So I was born in the Soviet Union.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:36.440
- Okay.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:39.680
- Which has had a huge
impact on my life because, A,

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.290
it makes me disproportionately
in love with America

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:44.941
and all its opportunities and I think it's

00:01:44.941 --> 00:01:47.290
grossly-underrated even
through a lot of people

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:48.213
watching think it's a risk.

00:01:48.213 --> 00:01:50.740
I think it's better than we even realize.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.640
B, it means I grew up with very little.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:53.660
We came to the US.

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:56.220
It was the late 70s,
the Jimmy Carter years.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:57.660
The economy collpsed.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:00.400
And we lived in a studio apartment

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.280
not bigger than this area that we're in

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.090
with six, seven, eight family members,

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:05.420
depending on the timing.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:06.870
So it was very humble beginnings,

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.110
but a very loving family.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:09.900
I have an all-time mom.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.210
I'm sure, through your research,

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:13.470
you've come to realize she's
disproportionately my hero

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:15.160
and I give her unbelievable
amounts of credit

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:16.083
for who I am.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:20.260
I was a super-entrepreneurial kid.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:22.910
You know, it's very rare
for somebody growing up

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:27.170
in the 80s who's an immigrant
to not be blindly bought

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.300
into education as the way out.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.360
Miraculously, I have such a great mother

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:35.630
who saw a four or five, I mean, young;

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:38.240
five, six, seven, eight year old kid

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:41.470
basically spend all his time on business.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:43.040
Lemonade.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:46.233
You know, I was a four-season executive.

00:02:46.233 --> 00:02:48.470
In the Fall, I'm raking leaves.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.560
In the winter, I'm shoveling snow

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.890
and singing Christmas carols at your door.

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:54.960
I mean, I literally, I
don't talk about that a lot.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.203
I literally ran to
people's doors and be like,

00:02:57.203 --> 00:03:00.210
will you pay us a dollar
to sing Jingle Bells,

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:01.100
me and my choir.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:02.040
I mean, it was ludicrous.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:02.873
- And you're Jewish, right?

00:03:02.873 --> 00:03:03.706
- And I'm Jewish.

00:03:03.706 --> 00:03:06.403
I mean, talk about,
I'm entrepreneur first.

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:13.030
In the spring, I remember we
did a miniature gold course.

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:15.455
We did mini-fleamarkets.

00:03:15.455 --> 00:03:16.640
It was a garage sale.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.050
In the summer, it was hardcore
washing cars and lemonade.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:21.210
I was so passionate.

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.030
And then, very quickly, for
a lot of people watching,

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:24.400
they'll probably remember this;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:25.910
in the late 80s, early 90s,

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:28.890
the baseball card phenomenon
in America happened,

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:30.640
and I was at the forefront.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.257
Not only was I collecting
but, as a 12 year old,

00:03:33.257 --> 00:03:38.257
I'm in hotel, in malls,
in convention centers,

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:42.253
set up as a dealer with other
30, 40, 50 year old men,

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:44.640
and really cut my teeth there.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.730
And then, my 14th birthday came
and I, for whatever reason,

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:50.300
I guess, that's grownup in Russia-Land,

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.900
my dad, at this point, in parallel,

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:56.520
was also an entrepreneur,
a more significant one,

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:58.670
saved up all his money as a stock-boy

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:00.710
and then manager of a liquor store,

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:02.580
which is the job he got in America,

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:06.390
and bought a liquor
store and dragged me into

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:08.070
that liquor store in
Springfield, New Jersey,

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:09.000
when I was 14.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.270
And so, from eight-grade on, I worked.

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:15.740
And so, from 14 to 18,
my high school years,

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:17.460
I fell in love with wine-collecting.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:19.934
Because my dad had a
liquor store but it was

00:04:19.934 --> 00:04:23.070
an affluent area of Jersey.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.700
We were in a middle-class
town but everything

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:26.970
surrounding us was stronger,
and people would come,

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:28.460
and they would ask for wine.

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:31.410
And when I found out that
people collected wine,

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:33.081
that's when it clicked for me.

00:04:33.081 --> 00:04:34.790
Because I wanted to help
my family business badly.

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:37.400
I wanted to be part of
the family business.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:38.970
But I hated liquor and beer.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:39.803
It wasn't interesting.

00:04:39.803 --> 00:04:42.180
I didn't want to sell
Absolut vodka or Coors Light.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.020
- But it was a great intersection because

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:47.090
you were selling lemonade
as an early little boy,

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:48.400
and then, you got into baseball.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.690
So you knew there was a value in a thing.

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:51.890
That's why you collected.

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:53.300
So now, you're going to be selling--

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:54.133
- Beverages.

00:04:54.133 --> 00:04:55.786
- [Alison] Liquor, yeah, collectibles.

00:04:55.786 --> 00:04:57.830
- It's a great thought
that I haven't thought of.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:59.337
So next we're...

00:04:59.337 --> 00:05:00.170
(chuckling)

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:01.970
Yeah, and it clicked right away.

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:03.330
I became very passionate.

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:07.660
Went to college and, within
the first couple of weeks

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:09.810
of being in college, I saw the internet,

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:12.460
which was September of 1994.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:13.430
I went into a dorm room.

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:14.263
I saw it.

00:05:14.263 --> 00:05:15.280
It hit me.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:17.050
And, over the next year,
I became infatuated

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:18.410
with what it could be.

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:21.800
And, in 1996, I launched one of the first

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:22.780
e-commerce wine businesses,

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:25.356
one of the first two or three, four

00:05:25.356 --> 00:05:28.860
e-commerce wine businesses,
selling wine on the internet;

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:31.380
called winelibrary.com, which is the name

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:32.990
that I wanted to call the business.

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:35.090
We were called Shopper's Discount Liquors.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:37.450
So winelibrary.com was registered.

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:39.210
In 1998, I took over my dad's,

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:41.270
I joined the family
business and really became

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:42.940
the operator of the business,

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:44.550
because in my college years,

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:46.750
during the summer, especially, obviously,

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:49.200
it was clear to my dad
that I was a talented kid

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:50.840
and I was making things happen.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:52.919
People are driving from two hours away

00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:56.840
to buy $35,000 worth of wine,

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:58.890
and I'm 19 years old.

00:05:58.890 --> 00:05:59.723
I'm not drinking.

00:05:59.723 --> 00:06:03.290
But I was so outrageously
right about what wines

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:06.080
were getting hot, which wines
were getting big scores.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:07.660
If you're watching and you know wines,

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:11.170
90 points, 92 points, that matters a lot.

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:13.650
Built a reputation long
before the internet.

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:16.830
And, from 1998 to 2005,
in a seven-year window,

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:18.810
I build my dad's business from a business

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:20.140
that was doing $3 million a year

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:22.550
to a business that was
doing $60 million a year.

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:24.690
And that, obviously, was a big deal

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:26.090
for an immigrant family.

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:28.090
- I think it's a big
deal, also, that your dad

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:30.410
was so flexible and dynamic in thought.

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:31.860
Because, I think, as an immigrant,

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:33.191
what I've found is they're like,

00:06:33.191 --> 00:06:35.300
these are the rules we're going to follow.

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:39.510
Here's the goal and let's
not go against the grain

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:42.844
because it might cause
us to go back 10 steps.

00:06:42.844 --> 00:06:45.450
He sounds accepting of what you're doing.

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:49.840
- My dad is historically
underrated for giving me

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:50.963
the air cover.

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:54.790
The reason I say that is, look,

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:57.360
when you, you know, I want to give my dad

00:06:57.360 --> 00:06:59.380
unlimited credit because he deserves it.

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:00.620
But my dad's smart.

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:02.340
When you have a 16 year old son

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:05.230
who stands behind a register
but takes the initiative

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:07.240
to go through the entire Beverage Journal,

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:08.710
where you order stuff from,

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:13.710
finds a closeout of
sparkling grapefruit juice,

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:15.950
convinces the dad to buy it all;

00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:19.870
it comes, it looks like
it came out of a cave.

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:22.070
But then, figures out
how to merchandise it

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:23.855
and make signs and sell it.

00:07:23.855 --> 00:07:27.150
I gave my dad a lot of
reasons to give me air cover.

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:29.840
But, to your point, it
takes a level of humility

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:31.950
that most immigrants...

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:33.700
You want to get really scared,

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:35.480
and this is going to blow your mind DRock.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:37.490
DRock is the man that
films me all the time.

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:38.390
So this is being filmed--

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:39.570
- We have some many cameras here,

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:41.810
I don't know which way to look, so.

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:43.710
- The day I started my
dad's store permanently,

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:48.710
1998, May of 1998, my
dad was 44 years old.

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:54.263
I'm 43 as of this second.

00:07:55.650 --> 00:08:00.020
The fact, to think that
tomorrow my son or daughter

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:01.600
could be coming into VaynerMedia,

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:03.770
and I'm giving them the keys to run it,

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:04.920
is just laughable.

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:08.510
Not only did he give it
to me, the air cover.

00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:09.663
He was a child.

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:12.160
That was kid.

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:13.830
You know, there's 68 year old right now

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:14.830
that are running the family business,

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:16.510
that are not letting their 44 year old son

00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:18.190
take over yet.

00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:20.050
There's 81 year olds watching right now

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:22.360
where she's not letting
her son or daughter

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:23.395
take over the business.

00:08:23.395 --> 00:08:24.228
- Right.

00:08:24.228 --> 00:08:26.150
- And they're 60 years old.

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:29.250
And my dad was 44 years old.

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:31.321
And, look, he did a
lot of backend keeping.

00:08:31.321 --> 00:08:32.840
He was involved.

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:36.180
But I did all the buying.

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:37.860
I did all the marketing.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:39.310
I did all the hiring.

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:40.143
I did it.

00:08:40.143 --> 00:08:40.976
I did it.

00:08:40.976 --> 00:08:43.610
And so, he deserves an
enormous amount of credit.

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:47.240
And I think hundreds of
millions of family businesses

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:49.190
can learn a lot from my dad's strategy.

00:08:49.190 --> 00:08:51.810
- Let's get into VaynerMedia
and VaynerSports,

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:53.117
all of these things that--

00:08:53.117 --> 00:08:53.950
- [Gary] The Vayner--

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:55.370
- The Vayner Empire.

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:58.340
Also, the fact that,
by the time you're 60,

00:08:58.340 --> 00:08:59.450
you're just going to be G.

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:01.116
Because you keep shortening your name.

00:09:01.116 --> 00:09:02.990
(laughing)

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:04.680
I'm afraid you're going
to have nothing left.

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:06.280
Like, you've taken out the -chuk,

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:07.390
and now, you've taken out the -ayner.

00:09:07.390 --> 00:09:09.277
And now, you're just GaryVee.

00:09:09.277 --> 00:09:10.850
- If, in the next 17 years,

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:14.853
I'm able to market in a way
that I can trump Google's G

00:09:14.853 --> 00:09:17.560
with my G, I'm going to do okay.

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:21.860
In 2009, my brother graduated college.

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:23.800
We had decided at that point we were going

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:25.050
to do something together.

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:31.180
I, in 2006, started Wine Library TV,

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:34.170
which was a daily show
I put out on YouTube

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:35.860
of me tasting wine.

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:38.420
That exploded, changed
the course of my career

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:39.253
in many ways.

00:09:39.253 --> 00:09:40.890
One, it taught me that
I was a personality.

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:41.830
I didn't know that.

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:44.690
Two, it made my wine business explode.

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:47.930
Three, it made me go deeper
into what was called Web 2.0,

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:49.630
that now we call social media.

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:51.640
But these are slang terms
for the current state

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:52.513
of the internet.

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:56.880
That led me to getting
infatuated with Facebook

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:59.810
and Twitter, which led me
to investing in Facebook

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:02.610
and Twitter, and Tumblr
which, as you can imagine,

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:05.218
changed the course of my financial career.

00:10:05.218 --> 00:10:08.670
It led to me writing a
book about the internet

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:11.000
and building reputation
and businesses on it

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:13.180
called, Crush It!, which was a huge

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:15.010
New York Times Bestseller.

00:10:15.010 --> 00:10:17.020
Led to companies reaching
out to me and saying,

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:20.060
we're Pepsi and you have a
million followers on Twitter,

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:21.420
and we have 16.

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:22.300
That's weird.

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:23.340
We are ready.

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:26.300
And so, one day, I called
my brother and said,

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:28.850
ESPN just paid me $5,000
to come in for an hour

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:30.100
and talk about the internet.

00:10:30.100 --> 00:10:32.980
I'm like, we should start
a company around that.

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:35.500
And I knew that I wanted
to build on my strengths.

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:39.720
I knew at 2009 that I wasn't
a great liquor store operator.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:43.150
I was a great marketer, that I
could point that to anything.

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:44.900
And so, that's the
beauty of self-awareness

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:46.450
and a little bit of experience.

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:50.070
So we started VaynerMedia,
and the plan was

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:54.210
let's become an agency for brands

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:57.080
and let's build a great company.

00:10:57.080 --> 00:10:59.010
And then, let's really
use it for ourselves.

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:00.500
Because having clients, at some level,

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:02.180
was not our interest.

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:05.690
I thought at the time, by 2015, '16, '17,

00:11:05.690 --> 00:11:08.060
the economy would collapse again, a cycle.

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:09.660
Because we're just coming out...

00:11:10.833 --> 00:11:11.666
We're building up.

00:11:11.666 --> 00:11:13.313
We'll six, seven, eight years.

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:16.030
And then, when it collapses, hopefully,

00:11:16.030 --> 00:11:17.970
the company will be good, and I'll be able

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:19.470
to have money and we'll be able to buy

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:22.050
a business for a penny on the dollar

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:23.560
because that's what always happens

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:24.660
when the economy collapses.

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:26.130
But now, it's 2019.

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:28.050
The economy has not collapsed in a decade.

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:31.069
As a matter of fact, it's over-frothy,

00:11:31.069 --> 00:11:34.250
and the company's become enormous.

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:36.120
So the idea of building this small company

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:37.370
and then translating all he people

00:11:37.370 --> 00:11:39.410
to the new company is out the window.

00:11:39.410 --> 00:11:42.660
Now, much like Wine Library is doing well,

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:45.020
I want VaynerMedia to do well forever

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:49.340
even when I go and buy Snickers or Puma,

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:51.140
or whatever I'm up to in my next chapter,

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:52.800
which is going to be buying a business

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:53.653
and running it,

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:56.880
I want Vayner to do great and I think

00:11:56.880 --> 00:11:59.030
a lot about that these days.

00:11:59.030 --> 00:12:00.740
- And you want to own the Jets.

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:02.000
- I want to own the New York Jets.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:03.310
- Which, we have a lot in common there,

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:05.460
I'll just say, because
I grew up in Alabama.

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:08.310
I'm a huge Alabama fan,
Roll Tide, and, of course,

00:12:08.310 --> 00:12:10.830
love Joe Namath, who played for the Jets.

00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:11.663
That's it.

00:12:11.663 --> 00:12:12.496
That's the brige.

00:12:12.496 --> 00:12:13.329
- I love it.

00:12:13.329 --> 00:12:14.162
(laughing)

00:12:14.162 --> 00:12:15.350
That's about where the
bridge ends because,

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:19.110
unless Roll Tide, who
win almost every year,

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:20.230
we lose every year.

00:12:20.230 --> 00:12:21.063
- Yeah.

00:12:21.063 --> 00:12:21.896
- And so, there's a big difference.

00:12:21.896 --> 00:12:24.020
And, by the way, just
another little connection,

00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:27.570
Richard Todd was also
an Alabama quarterback

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:30.440
who seems to be the blond Joe Namath.

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:31.273
He's pretty good.

00:12:31.273 --> 00:12:33.367
He was my first quarterback
but he was no Joe Namath.

00:12:33.367 --> 00:12:34.590
(overlapping discussion)

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:36.500
- How does that aspiration
fuel what you do?

00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:38.010
Is it just, it it one of those things

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:40.090
you just keep, you've said it so long

00:12:40.090 --> 00:12:42.970
that know it's become sort
of a mantra on the side?

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:43.803
Is that really?'

00:12:43.803 --> 00:12:44.636
- Yeah.

00:12:44.636 --> 00:12:45.543
- Yeah.

00:12:45.543 --> 00:12:46.376
- The answer's yes to both.

00:12:46.376 --> 00:12:48.030
I mean, I, in somewhere
around third or fourth grade,

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:50.175
I don't remember exactly so
I don't want to make it up.

00:12:50.175 --> 00:12:52.180
But before middle
schools, I started walking

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:53.500
around grammar school
saying that I was going

00:12:53.500 --> 00:12:54.770
to buy the Jets.

00:12:54.770 --> 00:12:56.140
It was a very big deal.

00:12:56.140 --> 00:12:58.400
In middle school and high
school, it was the only thing

00:12:58.400 --> 00:12:59.660
I talked about.

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:01.143
So that's a long time ago.

00:13:03.640 --> 00:13:06.900
But I have created more
clarity over the last

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:09.940
two or three years because
I'm unbelievably passionate

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:11.823
about happiness, not about money.

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:14.750
As a matter of fact, I'm unbelievably

00:13:14.750 --> 00:13:16.300
not motivated by money.

00:13:16.300 --> 00:13:17.870
I've come to learn that about myself

00:13:17.870 --> 00:13:20.160
in the last half-decade
because I'm leaving

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:21.590
a lot of money on the table.

00:13:21.590 --> 00:13:24.900
I'm motivated by the process.

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:26.710
I'm motivated by waking up in the morning

00:13:26.710 --> 00:13:29.100
and being fired up because I'm in control

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:31.870
of my destiny and I get to do what I want.

00:13:31.870 --> 00:13:33.170
And that who I am.

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:34.710
I'm somebody who found what,

00:13:34.710 --> 00:13:36.510
and I knew it from the beginning.

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:37.343
I didn't realize it.

00:13:37.343 --> 00:13:39.990
But now, looking back,
as we set up earlier,

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:42.980
my early actions spoke
to what my passion was.

00:13:42.980 --> 00:13:44.670
I always wanted to be in business.

00:13:44.670 --> 00:13:47.040
I always wanted to sell
from the beginning,

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:48.100
from the womb.

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:50.040
And I love the chase.

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:51.670
I love the process.

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:53.540
- Which is why you're
the serial entrepreneur.

00:13:53.540 --> 00:13:54.401
- [Gary] 100%.

00:13:54.401 --> 00:13:55.660
- You've got to keeping building.

00:13:55.660 --> 00:13:58.340
- Real entrepreneurs, which
is not what we have now.

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:01.310
We have a generation of fake entrepreneurs

00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:03.468
because entrepreneurship is cool.

00:14:03.468 --> 00:14:05.760
And I've benefited from that.

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:08.550
But I will not be a hypocrite or milk it.

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:09.730
I will speak the truth.

00:14:09.730 --> 00:14:12.600
And the truth is, entrepreneurship
is extremely hard.

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:14.210
It's outrageously lonely.

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:15.920
Almost everybody fails.

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:17.330
And a lot of people right now that say

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:19.000
they're entrepreneurs
on Instagram are going

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:20.890
to not be entrepreneurs in three years

00:14:20.890 --> 00:14:22.520
when that's not the cool thing,

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:24.180
when the economy collapses;

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:27.110
and I'm worried about
that because it leads

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:30.060
to depression and anxiety
and even the worst

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:31.280
of worsts, suicide.

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:35.070
And so, yes, that is why
I am an entrepreneur.

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:36.800
And that's why it's very easy for me

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:38.930
to know we're not living through an era

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:40.340
of real entrepreneurship,

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:42.530
because so many people that
I see are entrepreneurs

00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:44.269
are actually executives.

00:14:44.269 --> 00:14:45.910
- Do you think a show like Shark Tank

00:14:45.910 --> 00:14:48.010
has been good for the
entrepreneurial spirit

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:48.960
or has it--

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:49.793
- [Gary] Both.

00:14:49.793 --> 00:14:50.626
- Sort of--

00:14:50.626 --> 00:14:51.874
- You're barking, yeah, of course.

00:14:51.874 --> 00:14:54.552
- And has it made people
assume anybody can do it?

00:14:54.552 --> 00:14:55.700
- It's been both.

00:14:55.700 --> 00:14:57.060
What I like about Shark Tank is they show

00:14:57.060 --> 00:14:58.540
a lot of people not getting a deal.

00:14:58.540 --> 00:14:59.610
Thank God.

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:01.190
You know, what's bad about--

00:15:01.190 --> 00:15:02.350
- I saw you on Shark Tank, by the way.

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:04.287
- Oh, yeah, I saw that
I flashed (mumbling).

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:09.370
It's kind of, you know,
I also am very passionate

00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:10.400
about garage-sale-ing

00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:13.890
The reason I don't like
all these flipping shows

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:17.150
and storage shows is it's
just a highlight reel.

00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:18.710
Let me tell you, actually, what happens

00:15:18.710 --> 00:15:21.290
if you go storage auction hunting.

00:15:21.290 --> 00:15:23.627
99 of a 100 of them is a disaster

00:15:23.627 --> 00:15:27.060
and you just paid $83 for dog poopy.

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:28.410
And then, you've got to clean it out.

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:30.210
You've paid to work.

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:31.043
- Right.

00:15:31.043 --> 00:15:34.070
- So what Shark Tank has at least done

00:15:34.070 --> 00:15:35.860
is show us, a lot of times, it's no.

00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:37.320
It's hard.

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:39.360
It's just meeting, after
meeting, after meeting,

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:41.310
after meeting, and work,
and work, and work.

00:15:41.310 --> 00:15:46.310
And so, it's unbelievably
lonely being a number one.

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:49.290
I'm worried about what's
happening in London,

00:15:49.290 --> 00:15:52.100
LA, New York, right
now, and the well-being

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:54.110
of 900 employees.

00:15:54.110 --> 00:15:55.890
Right now, I got an email on the way here

00:15:55.890 --> 00:15:57.880
that the head of HR sent
something to somebody

00:15:57.880 --> 00:16:01.730
that said, you and your
daughter are in our thoughts.

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:02.850
Now, I'm like, what happened?

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:03.683
I'm worried.

00:16:03.683 --> 00:16:05.300
Like, you have 900 children.

00:16:05.300 --> 00:16:07.180
Imagine having 900 children.

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:08.250
That's what it is.

00:16:08.250 --> 00:16:10.360
A great operator, a great entrepreneur,

00:16:10.360 --> 00:16:13.170
a great leader, a great
CEO, that's what you do.

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:14.400
You work for everybody else.

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:16.440
And when you're a great entrepreneur...

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:18.740
A CEO is a great leader in that time

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:20.270
but she can leave in three years and be

00:16:20.270 --> 00:16:21.830
a different CEO.

00:16:21.830 --> 00:16:24.210
This is my baby for life.

00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:26.610
This is why people struggle
with work-life balance

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:27.490
when they're entrepreneurs.

00:16:27.490 --> 00:16:29.383
Your business is the next child.

00:16:29.383 --> 00:16:31.223
I have three children.

00:16:32.241 --> 00:16:33.810
And that's the truth.

00:16:33.810 --> 00:16:35.530
And maybe it's not the
most politically-correct

00:16:35.530 --> 00:16:36.620
thing to say right now,

00:16:36.620 --> 00:16:38.470
and everybody wants
more work-life balance,

00:16:38.470 --> 00:16:41.590
but a real entrepreneur will
tell you dead in your face

00:16:41.590 --> 00:16:45.790
in her or his truest moment,
my business is my other child.

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:48.220
It is my family member, because it is.

00:16:48.220 --> 00:16:49.670
- Well, I'll tell you
what was very eye-opening

00:16:49.670 --> 00:16:52.245
for me is hearing a lot of you,

00:16:52.245 --> 00:16:55.330
hearing you speak a lot about,
and not just in one context,

00:16:55.330 --> 00:16:58.237
but over and over again,
about your work-life balance

00:16:58.237 --> 00:17:02.341
and, specifically, this idea
of breadth versus depth,

00:17:02.341 --> 00:17:05.860
and that you're unapologetic
about working 18 hours

00:17:05.860 --> 00:17:07.720
a day and all during
the week and, possibly,

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:08.810
not seeing your kids.

00:17:08.810 --> 00:17:11.800
But when you are off-work,
you are in family.

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:13.700
And when you are in
family, you are off-work.

00:17:13.700 --> 00:17:14.533
- That's right.

00:17:14.533 --> 00:17:15.870
- But people are judge-y about that.

00:17:15.870 --> 00:17:17.169
I mean, when people say--

00:17:17.169 --> 00:17:18.180
- People are super-judge-y.

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:19.045
- Right--

00:17:19.045 --> 00:17:20.480
- I'm judged here.

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:22.610
There's a movement of
people trying to make me

00:17:22.610 --> 00:17:25.180
the face of overworking.

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:27.420
There are people right now using my name

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:30.940
to make a point that
we're glorifying hard work

00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:33.220
because they're not spending a lot of time

00:17:33.220 --> 00:17:35.080
looking at my content and
realizing that I'm talking

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:36.710
about happiness.

00:17:36.710 --> 00:17:38.900
I'm talking about working hard to people

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:40.250
who are complaining.

00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:42.370
If you're unhappy, and they,

00:17:42.370 --> 00:17:44.500
this is 90% of the conversation.

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:47.590
If you say to me, I'm not happy.

00:17:47.590 --> 00:17:49.030
I can't find my passion.

00:17:49.030 --> 00:17:50.880
I don t like my job.

00:17:50.880 --> 00:17:52.780
I'm unhappy.

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:57.510
My answer is to you is
work to find your way out.

00:17:57.510 --> 00:17:59.510
Yes, I think, when you get home,

00:17:59.510 --> 00:18:02.270
no, you should not complain about it.

00:18:02.270 --> 00:18:03.720
You should not dwell.

00:18:03.720 --> 00:18:06.360
You should not watch
Netflix or play video games

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:08.550
to escape your unhappiness.

00:18:08.550 --> 00:18:10.970
You should try to start
an internet business

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:12.530
at night, because it's practical.

00:18:12.530 --> 00:18:14.920
Because your grandma didn't have this.

00:18:14.920 --> 00:18:17.640
And use it over a four-year period

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:21.030
to be able to go do that and quit your job

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:22.720
that you hate.

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:24.550
I don't tell people to work 18 hours a day

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:27.420
and burn out and have suicidal thoughts

00:18:27.420 --> 00:18:28.680
when they're happy.

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:31.607
I'm talking to people
that complain and dwell,

00:18:31.607 --> 00:18:33.943
and that is 90% of the market.

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:36.120
- And do nothing about it.

00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:38.710
- Correct, but complain and dwell.

00:18:38.710 --> 00:18:41.092
I'm an action-oriented dude.

00:18:41.092 --> 00:18:43.980
I just don't know any other reaction

00:18:43.980 --> 00:18:46.810
based on being an immigrant,
being kind of the man

00:18:46.810 --> 00:18:48.580
of the house, because my
dad worked every minute.

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:52.050
All I know is problem, solve.

00:18:52.050 --> 00:18:55.940
The thought of problem, cry, is completely

00:18:55.940 --> 00:18:57.140
unacceptable to me.

00:18:57.140 --> 00:18:58.463
That is what the masses do.

00:18:58.463 --> 00:19:00.720
- So we talk a lot about hard skills.

00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:02.220
What about the soft skills?

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:05.840
What about the skills that
I feel like we've lost

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:08.150
in every generation to a little bit extent

00:19:08.150 --> 00:19:10.820
because we're not interpersonal.

00:19:10.820 --> 00:19:13.230
Where we are relying on
technology to communicate.

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:15.320
Maybe, it's exposed up
but it also has allowed us

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:16.830
to go into our own silos and cocoons.

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:17.663
- Well, I don't know about that.

00:19:17.663 --> 00:19:19.710
I mean, I think it depends
on how you define it.

00:19:19.710 --> 00:19:22.340
So, to me, when I think about soft skills,

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:24.200
I think about things more like gratitude

00:19:24.200 --> 00:19:26.780
and empathy and kindness, EQ,

00:19:26.780 --> 00:19:29.110
really what I trade on, right?

00:19:29.110 --> 00:19:31.670
I would argue that technologies allow me

00:19:31.670 --> 00:19:33.940
to scale that.

00:19:33.940 --> 00:19:37.230
Why is a letter or a
telephone that was rotary

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:39.230
considered so much better
than our cellphones

00:19:39.230 --> 00:19:41.066
that are on us at all times, right?

00:19:41.066 --> 00:19:42.740
The fact that, I'm looking at DRock.

00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:45.470
The fact that I can text him a heart emoji

00:19:45.470 --> 00:19:47.490
and he can know that
I'm thinking about him

00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:50.360
and I appreciate him,
and I'm using soft skills

00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:53.250
to show him gratitude,
yeah, I just don't agree.

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:54.320
I don't agree that--

00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:56.870
- But is that heart
emoji gonna replace a hug

00:19:56.870 --> 00:19:58.340
for your child?

00:19:58.340 --> 00:20:00.839
- No, but I'm not there.

00:20:00.839 --> 00:20:02.041
- I get that--

00:20:02.041 --> 00:20:03.100
(overlapping discussion)

00:20:03.100 --> 00:20:05.882
- No, no, let's stick
on that for a minute.

00:20:05.882 --> 00:20:06.715
(laughing)

00:20:06.715 --> 00:20:07.548
Is it going to replace it?

00:20:07.548 --> 00:20:08.420
Of course not.

00:20:08.420 --> 00:20:10.320
But what about when you're not there?

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:12.157
What about when you're
not thinking about it?

00:20:12.157 --> 00:20:15.610
Again, why do we put some people,

00:20:15.610 --> 00:20:17.570
you're talking semantics.

00:20:17.570 --> 00:20:18.960
And when people talk semantics,

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:20.990
they try to put things
like a written letter

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:22.280
on a pedestal.

00:20:22.280 --> 00:20:23.550
- Yeah, but I'm also talking that

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:26.636
I really couldn't care less
if my kids know calculus.

00:20:26.636 --> 00:20:27.650
(overlapping chatter)

00:20:27.650 --> 00:20:29.610
But I do care, and we have,

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:31.400
since the day they could walk and talk,

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:33.690
that they shake someone's
hand and look them in the eye.

00:20:33.690 --> 00:20:37.180
- Right, and I have 4,000
times in the last six months,

00:20:37.180 --> 00:20:39.620
made fun of people that
put that on a pedestal.

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:40.840
Not because I don't agree.

00:20:40.840 --> 00:20:42.060
I agree too much.

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:43.410
You and I are kindred spirits.

00:20:43.410 --> 00:20:44.680
That's probably why you've
been consuming so much

00:20:44.680 --> 00:20:45.590
of my content.

00:20:45.590 --> 00:20:46.670
I can tell already.

00:20:46.670 --> 00:20:47.850
I knew it.

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:50.610
It's that I don't want,
what you're talking about

00:20:50.610 --> 00:20:53.140
is respect, kindness, and empathy

00:20:53.140 --> 00:20:54.330
for another human being.

00:20:54.330 --> 00:20:55.163
- Yes.

00:20:55.163 --> 00:20:57.780
- You've decided that
a handshake and looking

00:20:57.780 --> 00:21:00.290
in the eye is the manifestation of that.

00:21:00.290 --> 00:21:03.650
I don't believe that to be true or needed

00:21:03.650 --> 00:21:05.800
in a world where things evolve.

00:21:05.800 --> 00:21:09.710
I think the spirit of it is
more important than ever,

00:21:09.710 --> 00:21:11.400
and always, actually, not more,

00:21:11.400 --> 00:21:13.500
is always the most important.

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:16.306
But I am not going to require my children

00:21:16.306 --> 00:21:19.650
to manifest it in what the correct state

00:21:19.650 --> 00:21:22.030
of a politically-correct version of it is.

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:24.250
I expect them to act on it.

00:21:24.250 --> 00:21:27.006
What I learned about
life is I've no interest

00:21:27.006 --> 00:21:29.790
in trying to change your mind.

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:31.990
I love that every
family, and every parent,

00:21:31.990 --> 00:21:34.080
and every little group of individuals

00:21:34.080 --> 00:21:38.040
gets to decide what those
little trinkets are of it.

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:41.600
But I have no passion for my
children to be entrepreneurs.

00:21:41.600 --> 00:21:42.433
- Right.

00:21:42.433 --> 00:21:43.266
- I have a passion for my children

00:21:43.266 --> 00:21:46.650
to be happy the way I am
because I'm an entrepreneur

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:48.270
and I was meant to be one for them.

00:21:48.270 --> 00:21:49.740
And if that's an artist, or a parent,

00:21:49.740 --> 00:21:52.110
or a student, or a professor;

00:21:52.110 --> 00:21:55.130
and I think that's how I think
about the handshake thing.

00:21:55.130 --> 00:21:57.780
I don't need to be
handshake-and-the-eye kid.

00:21:57.780 --> 00:22:00.580
I need them to manifest kindness, respect,

00:22:00.580 --> 00:22:02.060
caring, and things of that nature

00:22:02.060 --> 00:22:05.110
and I think those rules evolve.

00:22:05.110 --> 00:22:06.210
Those rules evolve.

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:08.770
Your great-great-grandmother
thinks it's insane

00:22:08.770 --> 00:22:10.904
that you're wearing pants right now.

00:22:10.904 --> 00:22:12.740
- She was pretty cool.

00:22:12.740 --> 00:22:15.358
- But that's not what a
lady does in her rules

00:22:15.358 --> 00:22:16.427
of you being a lady.

00:22:16.427 --> 00:22:18.032
But I think you're a lady.

00:22:18.032 --> 00:22:19.430
I mean that.

00:22:19.430 --> 00:22:21.453
The Beatles' hair was unacceptable.

00:22:22.910 --> 00:22:24.320
It just was.

00:22:24.320 --> 00:22:28.310
And so, I'm not willing to take historic

00:22:28.310 --> 00:22:33.310
or current social norms that
are the indicator of respect.

00:22:34.150 --> 00:22:36.250
I actually want my kids to respect people.

00:22:37.270 --> 00:22:38.498
- On that note,

00:22:38.498 --> 00:22:39.660
(laughing)

00:22:39.660 --> 00:22:40.550
thanks for being on the show.

00:22:40.550 --> 00:22:41.632
- Thanks for having me.

00:22:41.632 --> 00:22:44.299
(upbeat music)

